course: biology
lesson: 384
title: Mammal giants of the past: Paraceratherium, Diprotodon, the giant beaver and Gigantopithecus
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Picture an animal so tall that a giraffe could almost walk under its belly. That was Paraceratherium, a hornless relative of rhinos that browsed on treetops in Asia, including China, about 30 million years ago.
explain: Other mammal giants came later: a wombat-like animal the size of a hippo in Australia, a beaver as big as a black bear in North America, and a giant ape in the forests of southern China.
explain: A big body keeps heat in, can live on poor food like twigs and is hard for predators to kill. But it also needs huge amounts of food every day.
explain: When climates changed, forests shrank and, in some places, people arrived and hunted, so many giants died out. Today's African elephant is the last of many giant land mammals.
explain: Often scientists have only teeth or a few bones, so they say honestly what they know and what is still a guess, like the true height of the giant ape.
fact: Paraceratherium was a hornless relative of rhinos in Asia, including China, about 30 million years ago, as heavy as three or four elephants and one of the biggest land mammals ever.
fact: Like rhinos, Paraceratherium walked on three toes on each foot; a horse has one toe on each foot and an elephant five inside its round foot.
fact: Diprotodon was the biggest marsupial ever, a pouched relative of wombats the size of a hippo, living in Australia until about 40,000 years ago.
fact: Diprotodon was still alive when people reached Australia, and whether people helped it die out is still argued.
fact: The name Diprotodon means two forward teeth: two big front teeth stuck forward from its lower jaw, good for biting off plants.
fact: The giant beaver of North America was about two metres long, lived until about 12,000 years ago and is reported to have eaten mostly water plants, so it may not have built dams.
fact: In 1935 a scientist found huge ape teeth sold as dragon teeth in Hong Kong shops, and caves in southern China later gave many more.
fact: Gigantopithecus is known only from teeth and a few lower jaws, so its size is a guess; it was an orangutan relative often drawn about three metres tall.
fact: Gigantopithecus died out a few hundred thousand years ago when its forests changed and fruit grew scarce, and there is no good evidence it lives on as a Yeti or Bigfoot.
fact: Deinotherium was an elephant relative with two tusks growing from its lower jaw, pointing down and back; nobody is sure what they were for.
fact: A big body keeps heat in and is hard to attack, but it needs a lot of food, and giants have few babies.
exercise: What kind of animal was Paraceratherium? | A hornless relative of rhinos | A giant giraffe from the savanna | A long-necked dinosaur | A huge camel
exercise: How heavy was Paraceratherium? | As heavy as three or four elephants | As heavy as a horse | As heavy as a car tyre | As heavy as a whale shark ten times over
exercise: Why would a giant plant-eater need a long neck as well as long legs? | To reach leaves high in the trees | To see over the sea | To fight with its neck only, like giraffes | To drink from deep wells
exercise: How many toes did Paraceratherium have on each foot? | Three, like a rhino | One, like a horse | Five, like an elephant | Two, like a camel
exercise: How many toes does a horse have on each foot? | One | Three | Five | Four
exercise: What was Diprotodon? | The biggest marsupial ever, a giant wombat relative | A huge kangaroo that could fly | A giant rat from Europe | A kind of hippo from Africa that swam all the way to Australia
exercise: How would a mother Diprotodon carry her baby? | In a pouch | On her back like a sloth | In her mouth like a crocodile | In a nest of sticks
exercise: What does the name Diprotodon mean? | Two forward teeth | Terrible lizard | Giant wombat with tusks | Big foot
exercise: What did the giant beaver mostly eat, scientists reported? | Water plants | Fish and frogs | Big tree trunks | Other beavers
exercise: How was Gigantopithecus first found by scientists? | Its teeth were sold as dragon teeth in shops | A whole skeleton was dug out of the ice in Siberia | Someone filmed it in a forest | A farmer kept one as a pet
exercise: Why is the height of Gigantopithecus only a guess? | It is known only from teeth and a few jaws | It was always hiding from people in the thick forest | It shrank in old age | The bones were too big to measure
exercise: Which living ape is Gigantopithecus related to? | The orangutan | The gorilla only | The chimpanzee only | No living ape at all
exercise: Why did Gigantopithecus die out, scientists think? | Its forests changed and its fruit became scarce | It was hunted to the end by dinosaurs and sabre-toothed cats | It froze in a sudden snowstorm | It moved to America
exercise: Is there good evidence that Gigantopithecus lives on as Bigfoot? | No | Yes, many clear photos | Yes, bones found last year | Yes, in every forest
exercise: Where did Deinotherium's tusks grow from? | Its lower jaw, pointing down and back | Its forehead, like a rhino's horn | Its upper jaw, curving up and out like a mammoth | Its nose
exercise: Which is today's biggest land animal? | The African elephant | The giraffe | The hippo | The white rhino of Africa
test: Count them: a rhino has 3 toes on each foot. How many toes on all four feet? | Twelve | Three | Four | Sixteen
test: Count them: 2 horses and 1 rhino. How many toes touch the ground in all? | Twenty | Eight | Twelve | Sixteen
test: «Bigfoot's real, that giant ape is just hiding» What would you need before believing it? | Real bones, bodies or clear tested evidence | One blurry video from a mate that went viral | A story you heard twice | A big footprint in mud
test: In the Ice Age cartoon films giant beasts chat around. A friend says the giant beaver cut down huge forests. What is right? | It probably ate mostly water plants and may not have built dams | Yes, it cut down whole forests with its huge teeth to build giant dams | It ate only meat | It lived in the sea
test: «why'd a beaver that eats pond weeds need a dam lol» What is the best reasoning? | It found its food in water already, so it might not need one | Every beaver must build a dam, or it cannot live at all | Dams are for sleeping only | Beavers build dams to hide fish
test: Count them: a Deinotherium and a modern African elephant. How many tusks in all? | Four | Two | Six | Eight
test: «big animals are best, they win everything» What problem does a giant body bring? | It needs huge amounts of food every day | It cannot keep warm | Every predator can catch it | It cannot eat twigs
test: What would happen to a giant ape if the fruit forest around it turned into dry grassland? | It would struggle to find enough food | It would start eating grass at once | It would grow wings and leave | It would get bigger
test: «Diprotodon = two noses right?» What is right? | No, it means two forward teeth | Yes, two noses | No, it means two pouches for babies | No, it means giant wombat
test: Why might people arriving in Australia have mattered to Diprotodon? | Some scientists think hunting helped it die out, which is argued | People kept them all as pets | People taught them to swim | It had died out millions of years before people
test: Which is true about a big body? | It keeps heat in and is hard for predators to kill | It needs much less food than a small body does | It makes babies very quickly | It lets an animal hide easily
exam: Ascertain the number of toes upon each foot of Paraceratherium. | Three | One | Five | Two
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the discovery of Gigantopithecus. | Its teeth were first found among dragon teeth for sale in Hong Kong | A complete skeleton was recovered from glacial ice in the mountains of Tibet | It was photographed in the forests of North America | It was described from living specimens
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the stature of Gigantopithecus remains conjectural. | It is known solely from teeth and a small number of lower jaws | Its skeleton has been measured but disputed for political reasons | It changed in height with the seasons | Its bones are too large for any museum
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet attributed to the giant beaver? | It is reported to have subsisted principally upon aquatic plants | It consumed exclusively the trunks of great trees, which it felled itself | It was a predator of fish and frogs | It fed upon other rodents
